  stubbed API call) and a tiny Node CommonJS module ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, a [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo). No settings UI, no
  storage layer, no tests, no dependencies.

  [1mPath: architectural.[0m Preferences storage is a new subsystem this repo has no existing flow for, so I'll work through
  questions → approaches → a design → a spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m, rather than going straight to code. Say the
  word if you'd rather I just do the smallest possible thing inline.

  First fork, and it's the one that determines everything else: [1mwhere the preferences live.[0m

  - [1mBrowser-local ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m) in the webapp[0m — buys you persistence with zero infrastructure and works with the static
    page as it exists today. Costs: settings are per-browser/per-device, and anyone on that machine can read and edit
    them. Moving to server-backed later means writing the API and a migration, but the module's read/write interface can
    stay the same, so that switch is moderately cheap if I design for it.
  - [1mServer-backed, keyed by user[0m — buys cross-device persistence and true per-account settings. Costs: there is no
    server here. [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns a fake success, so this means building or mocking a backend
    and deciding auth first. Not cheap to reverse into, and it's a much bigger piece of work.
  - [1mNode-side config file on disk[0m — persistence for the [38;5;153msrc/[39m entry point, not the webapp. Only makes sense if "sessions"
    means CLI runs, not browser visits.

  I recommend browser-local [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, behind a small preferences module with a narrow interface ([38;5;153mget[39m / [38;5;153mset[39m /
  [38;5;153msubscribe[39m), so the storage backend is swappable later without touching callers. It matches the one real app surface
  that exists and doesn't require inventing a backend.
